\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Street Tree Roots and Sidewalk Damage:\\A Four-District Survey}
\author{T.~Okafor}
\date{2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Root-related sidewalk damage is among the most frequent tree-related
claims against municipal budgets \cite{randrup2001}.
Surveys in comparable climates tie damage risk to species choice and
planting pit size \cite{mullaney2015}.
This survey quantifies how much of the damage in four districts is
attributable to the two dominant species.

\section{Methods}
We walked every block in four districts and logged each lifted or
cracked panel with the nearest tree.
Species, pit dimensions, and panel offset were recorded for 612 damage
sites.

\section{Results}
Two species account for 71 percent of logged damage sites, and pit area
predicts damage better than tree age \cite{luk2010}.
Narrow pits under one square meter are associated with the highest
damage density \cite{konijnendijk2008}.

\section{Discussion}
Widening planting pits is cheaper than repeated panel replacement, and
species selection at planting time dominates long-run cost.
The next survey round will test root barriers on the worst corridors.

